#! /bin/sh
patch -p1 -f -l $* < $0
exit $?

From: Thomas Friedrichsmeier <thomas.friedrichsmeier@gnupg.com>

Qt applies a default activation policy of NSApplicationActivationPolicyRegular,
which makes the app appear like a regular GUI application, including automatic
addition to the Dock while running, and as a recently used item.

Using NSApplicationActivationPolicyAccessory for helper applications, instead,
hides those from the Dock, and makes them appear modal to the spawing app.

The latter is exactly what we want for pinentry-qt. For this, we patch Qt
itself, as this might be applicable to further helper apps, but also because
we do not have solid control over when activation policy gets applied,
otherwise.
--- a/src/plugins/platforms/cocoa/qcocoahelpers.mm	2026-06-04 18:29:11
+++ b/src/plugins/platforms/cocoa/qcocoahelpers.mm	2026-11-11 12:03:34
@@ -175,7 +175,11 @@
     }

     if (forceTransform) {
-        [[NSApplication sharedApplication] setActivationPolicy:NSApplicationActivationPolicyRegular];
+        if (qApp->applicationDirPath().endsWith(QStringLiteral("/MacOS"))) {
+            [[NSApplication sharedApplication] setActivationPolicy:NSApplicationActivationPolicyRegular];
+        } else {
+            [[NSApplication sharedApplication] setActivationPolicy:NSApplicationActivationPolicyAccessory];
+        }
     }
 }
